Let async shader compilation never build a pipeline on the emulation thread (core option)

With async_shader_compilation on and patches 0005 and 0006, an iPhone 14 Pro
(Majora's Mask 3D, Low Power Mode, 2026-10-19) still stopped 0.35 to 0.97 s
at a new scene, each stop right after a MoltenVK "Compilation succeeded".
Two paths still compile on the emulation thread:

- GraphicsPipeline::TryBuild first asks the driver for the pipeline with
  FAIL_ON_PIPELINE_COMPILE_REQUIRED (Build(true)). MoltenVK honours that flag
  only for the SPIR-V to MSL step: when the MSL library is already in its
  pipeline cache (from the disk driver cache, or another pipeline using the
  same shader), it goes on and compiles the Metal pipeline state and waits for
  it. The probe also takes MVKPipelineCache's shader-cache lock, which a worker
  holds for the whole of a compile.
- Draws whose vertices are processed in software (geometry shaders, triangle
  fans) bind their pipeline with wait_built = true always
  (RasterizerVulkan::Draw), so they wait for any compile.

This adds the option `citra_async_never_waits` (default disabled, so a frontend
that does not set it sees no change). When it and async_shader_compilation are
on, a pipeline that is not built yet is queued to the pipeline workers without
the driver probe, and software-vertex draws are skipped until it is ready, as
accelerated draws already are. The cost is the same as 0006's: an object can
miss a frame or two the first time. Pipelines in the disk shader cache are
still built on the workers at boot (ShaderDiskCache::InitPLCache).

